# converted from 01-eleven-miles.md
@title Eleven Miles
@bg eastline
@mood grief

narrate Twelve years before the boy came into the Mire, a healer named Cael picked up his daughter and started walking.
narrate She was seven. She weighed almost nothing. He would think about that afterwards — not the walk, not the gate, not any of the things people assumed he thought about. He would think: she weighed almost nothing, and I carried her eleven miles, and I did not once have to put her down.
narrate That was the last good fact of his life and he did not know it yet.
narrate She had been ill for seven days.
narrate He knew what it was on the second. He was a hedge healer — the only one in forty miles who would treat a person without being paid first — and he had seen the Grey Fever four times, and he knew the grey at the fingertips and the cough that was not quite a cough, and he knew the arithmetic.
narrate On the fourth day he stopped lying to her.
corin neutral Am I going to die?
bryn neutral Probably,
narrate She thought about that. She was seven and she thought about it seriously, the way she thought about everything, with her head tilted.
corin neutral Are you sad?
bryn neutral Yes.
corin neutral Don't be.
corin neutral You're always sad. It's your face.
narrate He laughed, and she was pleased, and he went into the other room and stood with his hands on the table for a long time.
narrate On the seventh day he wrapped her in his coat and walked out.
narrate There was a checkpoint eleven miles east. He had been turned back from it twice in his life, both times for nothing, both times by men who were polite about it. There was a physician behind that line. There was medicine behind that line — real medicine, the kind the Order kept for its own, the kind that cost more than a Mire village earned in a year.
narrate He did not go there to beg. He would want that understood, later, when people told the story. He went there because there was a girl in his arms and there was a thing that might save her and it was eleven miles away, and not walking eleven miles would have been a decision, and he was not prepared to make it.
narrate So he walked.
narrate He talked the whole way. That was for her and it was also for him.
bryn neutral Do you remember the river.
corin neutral Mm.
bryn neutral You fell in. You were four. You went in sideways and came up furious — you didn't cry, you were so angry. You stood in the shallows and you told me it was the river's fault.
corin neutral It was.
bryn neutral It was.
narrate She laughed — that small cracked laugh, the one she had, the one he would not be able to hear for the rest of his life — and put her head against his shoulder, and went quiet.
narrate He walked eleven miles.
narrate He arrived at dusk.
narrate The gate was a rope between two posts and a man behind it with a ledger.
narrate He would learn the man's name later. He would learn it, and hold it, and turn it over for twelve years the way other men turn over a stone in their boot. But at the time he was just a sergeant, grey, tired, competent, and he looked at Cael the way you look at a thing you have to make a decision about.
corin neutral Fever?
bryn neutral Yes.
corin neutral Then you can't come through.
bryn neutral She's seven.
corin neutral Then she's seven and she can't come through.
narrate Cael had prepared for this. That was the thing. He had walked eleven miles and he had prepared, and he had four arguments and two threats and a story about the Fever being dormant in children, which was true, and which he had tested, and which he could have explained if the man had given him ninety seconds.
narrate He used none of it.
narrate Because somewhere around the ninth mile he had understood something, and the understanding had arrived the way water arrives in a boat — not at a hole, but through the whole hull at once — and what he had understood was this:
narrate He could have shouted. He had the breath for it.
narrate Instead he asked the man his name.
corin neutral Bryn,
corin neutral Bryn.
corin neutral I want you to remember that I asked you nicely.
narrate Cael adjusted his daughter's weight — she had gone heavy in the way that sleep makes a child heavy, and she was asleep, and she would sleep the whole way back, which was a mercy he did not recognise as one
narrate And then he turned around.
narrate He walked eleven miles back.
narrate He arrived at dawn. He put her in her bed. He lit the fire, because she would wake up cold and he wanted the room warm, and he sat in the chair beside her bed — the chair with the terrible cushion on it, the one she had made for him — and he watched her breathe for four hours.
narrate She woke at midday.
bryn neutral Did we go somewhere?
corin neutral We did.
bryn neutral Was it good?
corin neutral ...No.
bryn neutral Oh.
bryn neutral Did you carry me the whole way?
corin neutral Every step.
narrate She nodded, satisfied, as though this confirmed something she had suspected about the world.
bryn neutral Good,
narrate That night, Cael went outside and looked north.
narrate There was a place, eleven miles north of the Hollow, in a fold of ground where the Mire stopped being wet and started being something else. Every child in the Mire knew about it. Every adult in the Mire pretended not to.
narrate A hole in a field. Grass right up to the edge.
narrate He had told Ilsa a story about it once — that it was where the Mire kept its secrets, and that if you were very good and very quiet you could hear them — and she had asked him what secrets, and he had said the important ones, and she had believed him, because she was six and he was her father and he had never once lied to her about anything that mattered.
narrate He stood in his garden in the dark and looked north for a long time.
narrate And then he went inside, because she would wake up soon, and she would want the fire, and he wanted to be there when she did.
narrate Not yet, he told himself.
